2️⃣ Kubernetes Deployment Broke Production

Context

New deployment rolled out using CI/CD

Change involved config map / env var / image tag

Impact

Pods entered CrashLoopBackOff

Partial outage

Detection

Alert on pod restart count

Logs showed missing config / permission error

Action

Immediate rollback

Disabled pipeline auto-deploy

Used kubectl describe & logs

Resolution

Service restored within minutes

Learning

“We added canary deployments and mandatory config validation in CI. Rollbacks became automated instead of manual.”
___________________________________________________________________________--

Storytelling answer:

As a standard practice, we use canary deployments for application code changes to limit blast radius and
prevent major outages.

In this incident, a deployment went through our CI/CD pipeline that involved only configuration changes, 
specifically updates to a ConfigMap and environment variables. At that time, 
canary safeguards were not applied to configuration-only deployments, and the updated configuration was rolled out 
to all pods simultaneously.

Shortly after the deployment, several pods began entering a CrashLoopBackOff state, 
leading to a partial service outage.
We were alerted by a sudden spike in pod restart counts. Upon investigation using 
application logs and kubectl describe,
we identified that a required configuration value was missing, causing the application to fail during startup.

The immediate mitigation was to roll back the configuration change, which restored service quickly. 
To prevent further impact, we temporarily disabled auto-deployments while we conducted a root cause analysis.

As a long-term preventive measure, we extended canary deployments to cover configuration changes, 
added configuration validation checks in CI to catch missing or invalid values, and implemented
automated rollback based on pod health indicators such as restart thresholds and readiness failures.
